Found by 8 age test kit vectors (t/07-testkit.t), all expect: payload failure:
stream_last_chunk_empty, stream_no_final_full, stream_no_final_two_chunks_full,
stream_trailing_garbage_short, stream_trailing_garbage_long, stream_two_final_chunks,
stream_two_final_chunks_full, stream_two_final_chunks_second.

One root cause in Crypt::Age::Primitives::decrypt_payload_fh: it decides a chunk is
final purely from

    my $ct = $class->paranoid_read($ifh, $max_encrypted_chunk);
    ...
    $is_final = eof($ifh);

i.e. 'did reading up to one chunk's worth of bytes happen to exhaust the filehandle',
with no lookahead and no check that the filehandle is actually exhausted once some
chunk has been accepted as final. Two symptoms follow:

(a) A chunk that reads as exactly a full 64 KiB+tag AND happens to end the file is
always decrypted with the final-flagged nonce, even when it was written with the
non-final nonce (a writer bug or, here, a deliberately malformed test vector) and
more data was meant to follow. The nonce mismatch fails Poly1305 authentication
immediately, so the chunk's plaintext is never printed to the output handle at all —
stream_no_final_full expects the full 64 KiB to have been released before the file
is flagged invalid (payload failure with partial release), but this library currently
releases 0 bytes. stream_no_final_two_chunks_full: expected 128 KiB released, actual
64 KiB (first chunk only).

(b) Once a chunk IS accepted as final, the while(!$is_final) loop just stops. It
never checks whether unread bytes remain. A spurious empty final chunk tacked on
after real data (stream_last_chunk_empty), or outright random trailing garbage after
a genuine final chunk (stream_trailing_garbage_short/long), or a second/third chunk
after one already accepted as final (stream_two_final_chunks*) all currently decrypt
as if the file ended cleanly at the first accepted final chunk — this library reports
success or the wrong partial-release length where the spec requires an error.

This is invisible to every existing round-trip test (unit or t/04-interop.t) because
Crypt::Age's own writer (encrypt_payload_fh) always marks its true last chunk final
and never appends anything after it — only a reader-robustness vector generated by
something else can reach this path, which is exactly what the test kit is for.

Not fixed here per the test-writer's lane (t/07-testkit.t asserts against these
vectors as normal, unmarked failures — no TODO, no weakened assertion). Cross-check
against the real Go reference's STREAM/stream.go read-ahead logic before attempting a
fix; this ticket's body is a diagnosis from black-box vector behaviour, not a verified
description of the correct algorithm.

