THE TICKET'S DIAGNOSIS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE CAUSE, WRONG ABOUT THE CURE.
Symptoms (a) and (b) as written are accurate. But the proposed fix -- decide
finality by looking ahead for remaining bytes -- cannot work, and the vectors
prove it two different ways:

  * stream_no_final_full is a single FULL chunk written with the NON-final
    nonce and nothing after it. Lookahead says 'nothing follows, so this is
    final', decrypts under the final-flag nonce, fails Poly1305 and releases 0
    bytes. The vector requires 64 KiB released and THEN an error.
  * stream_trailing_garbage_short/long is a correctly-marked FULL FINAL chunk
    with junk appended. Lookahead says 'bytes follow, so this is not final',
    decrypts under the non-final nonce, fails and releases 0 bytes. The vector
    requires the full 64 KiB released (its payload hash is byte-identical to
    the stream_last_chunk_full success vector) and THEN an error.

Those two demand opposite answers from the same lookahead signal, so no amount
of read-ahead decides finality. Finality is a property of the nonce the writer
authenticated the chunk under, and the only way to read that nonce is to
attempt authentication under it. The reference agrees: filippo.io/age
internal/stream/stream.go readChunk() tries the non-final nonce and, on
failure, retries with setLastChunkFlag -- it has no lookahead for finality at
all. Its only read-ahead is a 1-byte probe AFTER a chunk authenticated as
final, purely to detect trailing data.

ON THE PARTITIONING-ORACLE OBJECTION: checked against the spec text, and it
does not apply here. Every partitioning-oracle mention in c2sp.org/age (5 of
them) is about checking a STANZA BODY is exactly 32/1120 bytes before
decrypting it in a recipient type; the Payload section contains no such
warning. The retry is also not a second chance: it is the same key over the
same ciphertext under a different nonce, and no ciphertext can authenticate
under both, so it can never accept a chunk the writer did not mark that way.
It runs only when the first verification already failed, and the unauthenticated
output of decrypt_add is discarded unless decrypt_done passes.

ALGORITHM NOW IMPLEMENTED (mirrors the reference):
  - short read == end of file (paranoid_read only returns short there); a full
    read proves nothing either way.
  - short read: reject 0 bytes ('end of file reached without a final chunk',
    the spec's streaming-decryption MUST), reject < tag size, reject an empty
    final chunk unless it is chunk 0 (spec: final chunk MUST NOT be empty
    unless the whole payload is empty). Then it can only be final.
  - full read: verify under the non-final nonce; if that fails, verify under
    the final nonce. Common case stays at one AEAD pass per chunk; the second
    pass costs one extra chunk decryption only for a payload that is an exact
    multiple of 64 KiB, same as the reference.
  - after any chunk that authenticated as final: 1-byte read; any byte left in
    the file rejects it, trailing garbage and a well-formed extra chunk alike.

All new error strings keep the existing 'Payload authentication failed at chunk
N' prefix (with a ': reason' suffix). That prefix is what t/07-testkit.t
classifies a payload failure by, and it is what a real caller sees, so it is
effectively the public contract for this class -- changing it would have
silently reclassified vectors as header failures.

PARTIAL RELEASE IS NOW PART OF THE DOCUMENTED CONTRACT. decrypt_payload_fh
streams, so chunks that authenticated before an error have already reached the
caller's output handle. =method decrypt_payload_fh now states this explicitly:
each released chunk is individually authenticated, but the message as a whole
is not -- its completeness is precisely what the error says was never
established -- so a caller MUST discard what reached the handle when the call
dies. =method decrypt_payload was updated too: it buffers internally and
therefore releases nothing on failure, which is now the documented difference
between the two.

